1
00:00:00,978 --> 00:00:10,661
Welcome to Africa episode six of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:23,178
Five countries connect the Great Lakes with the Indian Ocean: Uganda, Kenya,
Rwanda, Burundi and Tanzania. This shorter lesson gives extra attention to the
small inland pair.

3
00:00:23,847 --> 00:00:27,260
Let's bring Uganda and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:30,991 --> 00:00:43,402
Uganda is landlocked west of Kenya and north of Tanzania and Rwanda. Its
southern side reaches Lake Victoria, while the Democratic Republic of the
Congo lies west.

5
00:00:44,056 --> 00:00:57,059
Bwindi’s forest in southwestern Uganda is habitat for mountain gorillas. The
protected landscape lies where lowland and mountain forests meet, supporting
rich plant and animal life.

6
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:06,254
Picture a gorilla forest west of Kenya and above Lake Victoria. Uganda has a
great lake shore but no ocean coastline.

7
00:01:08,633 --> 00:01:17,758
Kenya lies east of Uganda, below Ethiopia and Somalia, and above Tanzania. Its
southeastern coastline reaches the Indian Ocean.

8
00:01:18,422 --> 00:01:33,222
The Great Rift Valley lakes in Kenya support remarkable birdlife, including
large gatherings of flamingos. Different lake conditions create feeding places
for different species.

9
00:01:33,878 --> 00:01:44,536
Remember pink birds along the rift, with the Indian Ocean beyond the
southeast. Kenya is the coastal neighbour east of Uganda and north of
Tanzania.

10
00:01:45,237 --> 00:01:49,672
Let's bring Rwanda and its neighbours into view.

11
00:01:52,734 --> 00:02:03,055
Rwanda is the small landlocked country below Uganda, east of the Democratic
Republic of the Congo and above Burundi. Tanzania borders its eastern side.

12
00:02:03,707 --> 00:02:16,455
Nyungwe National Park preserves montane rainforest with chimpanzees and other
primates. Mountain forest is an important part of this country’s landscape,
despite its small area.

13
00:02:17,122 --> 00:02:26,932
Picture a forested highland patch above Burundi. Keep the small pair in order:
Rwanda north, Burundi south, both inland west of Tanzania.

14
00:02:29,248 --> 00:02:40,417
Burundi lies immediately below Rwanda, east of the Democratic Republic of the
Congo and west of Tanzania. Its southwestern edge reaches Lake Tanganyika.

15
00:02:41,081 --> 00:02:53,063
The ritual dance of the royal drum combines powerful ensemble drumming with
dance and song. This living cultural tradition makes the drum a memorable
symbol for Burundi.

16
00:02:53,726 --> 00:03:04,732
Hear the drum below Rwanda and beside Lake Tanganyika. Burundi is the southern
member of the small inland pair; neither country reaches the ocean.

17
00:03:05,403 --> 00:03:08,944
Let's bring Tanzania and its neighbours into view.

18
00:03:11,841 --> 00:03:22,290
Tanzania lies below Kenya and Uganda, east of Rwanda and Burundi, and north of
Mozambique. It reaches the Indian Ocean and includes Zanzibar offshore.

19
00:03:22,951 --> 00:03:36,384
On the Serengeti plains, great herds of wildebeest, zebras and gazelles move
in search of water and grazing. Predators follow this seasonal movement across
the wider ecosystem.

20
00:03:37,036 --> 00:03:47,949
Picture moving herds below Kenya, with Zanzibar offshore to the east. Tanzania
connects the Great Lakes region with the Indian Ocean coast.

21
00:03:52,028 --> 00:04:05,972
Uganda lies west of Kenya and above Lake Victoria. Rwanda is above Burundi,
both west of Tanzania. Tanzania lies below Kenya and reaches the Indian Ocean,
with Zanzibar offshore.

22
00:04:06,636 --> 00:04:24,511
Recall Uganda’s gorilla forest, Kenya’s rift-lake birds, Rwanda’s mountain
forest, Burundi’s drums and Tanzania’s moving herds. Great lakes give inland
countries shores, but those are not ocean coastlines.
